title encoding/pem.rye — PEM (RFC 7468) in pure Rye as a composition, parity-GREEN
prompt recur the road ahead Lindy-first crux-first, Rye prioritized, Glow on green-witnessed Rye; send each finished increment as its own signed round; stop at custody gates
think The crypto suite (27 files) and six encoding rungs (base64/58/58check/32/hex/bech32) all stand GREEN. Lindy-first crux-first: the README named a to_hex ratchet and Base32 variants, yet the to_hex ratchet is a broad multi-file sweep (against narrow-scope for an autonomous round) and the Base32 variants wait on a surface that needs them. The higher-Lindy, in-charter, single-file crux is PEM — base64.rye's own front door already names PEM as its purpose, and every Ed25519 key, certificate, and signed carry travels as PEM between hands, into config files, and beside code. It composes the already-GREEN standard Base64, so it is a clean composition like base58check, not new arithmetic.
think Authored pem.rye from RFC 7468: encode writes -----BEGIN <label>-----, the standard Base64 of the payload wrapped at 64 characters a line each ended by a newline, and -----END <label>-----; decode locates the BEGIN/END markers for the requested label, gathers every Base64 character between them (skipping the wrap whitespace RFC 7468 allows), and Base64-decodes the body. The key custody-honest move is decode refusing BadLabel when a block does not claim the requested label — bytes are never returned from a block of the wrong kind.
think Kept every buffer bound named (max_der_bytes inherited from base64, max_label_len 64), contract asserts on every function with positive // invariant lines, all counts u32, the marker lines built through one marker_line helper so BEGIN and END read from one law. Sized the exact output before the first write (begin_line + body + one newline per wrap line + end_line) and refused DestTooSmall ahead of any partial write.
think Parity landed the base58check way since Zig ships no std PEM: a reproducible RFC-4648-anchored known-answer (the block wrapping "foobar" under "GRAIN TEST" is exactly -----BEGIN GRAIN TEST----- ‖ Zm9vYmFy ‖ -----END GRAIN TEST-----, its body the published RFC 4648 §10 Base64 of "foobar"), a round-trip across payloads 0..96 bytes whose body lines are asserted never to exceed 64 characters, and a second independent deframer that splits the block on newlines and recovers the same body — a genuine cross-implementation check standing in for the missing std reference.
obs GREEN on the metal first build. A wrong label refuses BadLabel, a non-Base64 body character BadChar, a too-small destination DestTooSmall — each before a partial result escapes. tame_style_check bans clean; pem.rye carries asserts throughout (absent from the zero-assert and 70-line ratchets).
obs Non-gated — purely local arithmetic, no key, no network, no funds, no real device. The encoding module now stands at seven rungs, and the crypto stack has the labelled text armor a key wears end to end, composing the Season G Base64.
loom witness=encoding_pem_witness green=1 rfc4648_anchored_kat=1 roundtrip_bytes=0..96 wrap_width_asserted=64 independent_deframer=1
file encoding/pem.rye PEM encode/decode in pure Rye composing base64.rye, RFC 7468 framing + 64-char wrap, BadLabel refusal
file tools/encoding_pem_witness.rish per-file witness building fresh and asserting the GREEN encoding-pem line
file encoding/README.md seats pem as the seventh rung; notes its known-answer and independent-deframer parity
recommend keep-going dress a real crypto/ed25519 public key as a PEM PUBLIC KEY block end-to-end, or the to_hex ratchet migration onto hex.rye — both agent-doable, no gate
